== Can a non-zero divisor be declared? ==
ok  NotEqualZero                       accepted (expected accepted)
== ...and does it actually EXCLUDE zero, or is it a silent no-op? ==
ok  ZeroRejected                       refused (expected refused)
== Is a DISJOINT refinement the problem? (no -- this is accepted) ==
ok  DisjointOr                         accepted (expected accepted)
== It is the NEGATIVE LITERAL. All three of these are refused. ==
!!  NegSingle                          accepted (expected refused)
!!  NegAnd                             accepted (expected refused)
!!  NegOr                              accepted (expected refused)
== ...while a relational PATTERN reads the same literal fine. ==
ok  NegPattern                         accepted (expected accepted)

== The residuals the algebra hands back for a divisor ==
  unbounded int, minus zero : int <= -1 | int >= 1
  -10..10,       minus zero : -10..-1 | 1..10
  The second is the one that matters: a BOUNDED divisor's residual names a
  negative bound, and the probes above show the surface cannot spell it.

